  "statement": {
    "case_for": "The Aharonov–Bohm effect and Berry phase show that quantum phase carries observable physical consequences, an insight that became foundational across quantum physics. Both effects have extensive experimental confirmation and sustained influence. I favor this case because it joins conceptual depth with clear clues in nature.",
    "case_against": "The ballot combines historically distinct results. A single citation could blur their separate origins and leave the scope of the recognized achievement unclear.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson finds both contributions foundational and experimentally mature, while cautioning that the combined citation spans distinct contributions and needs careful definition.",
        "response": "I share that concern. The citation should name the physical significance of quantum phase as the connecting idea while identifying both effects explicitly; the shared theme makes the combination defensible, but does not erase their separate histories."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson emphasizes the broad historical development of the effects and questions whether the nomination's combined scope is sharply bounded.",
        "response": "That is a real attribution issue. It warrants a focused citation and careful expert review of the historical record, but the mature, experimentally established role of these two effects in showing the observability of phase remains compelling."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether a single prize citation can fairly encompass the distinct histories of the Aharonov–Bohm effect and Berry phase."
    ],
